[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There\'s been a pair of AR glasses that has been absolutely blowing up this month, the UR XR 1. So, of course, I had to go investigate and I spent a day fully testing these glasses. Now, just to illustrate how much they\'ve blown up, they raised $500,000'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the first 24 hours of their Kickstarter going live. Now, I did get to test these before at AWE 2026 for about 10 minutes a couple weeks ago, but today I get to do a private demo with a few other selected YouTubers to test'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these all day, ask as many questions as I can think of, and figure out if these are worth getting for me and for all of you guys. But first, we just finished announcing four different giveaways in'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the last 2 weeks, and I know you guys are really excited to find out who wins, but don\'t worry, you can still enter any of those four giveaways by watching those four videos and following the'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instructions. I will announce all four winners in one video and ship out all of the winnings to you guys. I\'m so excited. If you haven\'t yet, check out those last four videos. There\'s still a tiny bit of time left. Now, on to the UR'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'XR 1s. These were created by Unseen Realities, the head of which is CEO Edward Zhao. Now, many people compare the UR XR 1 as sort of a hybrid between the Apple Vision Pro and AR glasses plus'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some VR capabilities. And that\'s because the CEO actually worked on the Apple Vision Pro and on the XR team at Meta. So, huge inspiration came from both of those and is built into here. And this'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is not hidden. Just like the Apple Vision Pro, it has virtual see-through, meaning you\'re getting the real world in full brightness through a virtual display rather than an optical one or a real life one, which basically lets you'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have real transparent views of things. This makes it brighter in size, so you can see those around you as clear as you want, and you can even see all that outside. It makes it so you don\'t need a million nits of brightness just to see your floating screen. It\'s completely'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'crystal clear, completely fully opaque, and even provides a slight sunglass effect for outside, which are all huge advantages to all other AR glasses. As far as what these glasses will come with, they\'ll have the glasses here,'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just like you see, and this little case, both of which are smaller than I expected or remembered. The back of the case is actually magnetic, too. So, you can attach a phone, power bank, or the'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'adapter which I used in this video straight to the back of it. You\'re also getting the USB-C AR glasses cord, which is a welcome addition instead of some brands\' proprietary cords without USB-C, and that\'s about as much as we know of'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the unboxing experience. As for why you\'d actually want to get these, all the questions I had asked and that were answered from Steam VR to flexibility of the screens and the things that were actually wrong about these glasses that'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'weren\'t as good as they could have been, I\'m going to talk about all of that in this video. So, don\'t click off if you want to see all that. First, let\'s run through some specs here. The XR1 are VST XR glasses with a 5K dual eye display in'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'full color, so 2.5K per eye. That\'s much higher than all other AR glasses. It has a 90° field of view, which is double what many AR glasses are, and it\'s 20° larger than even the unreleased Project'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Aura from Xreal and Google. And it supports seven screens simultaneously. This can be all Windows screens on your laptop or all app screens via their software like your typical Android apps'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or any mix of the two. And you can place these screens anywhere with your device controls, whether that\'s the mouse and trackpad or with their full hand tracking. Now, on your phone with their software, you\'re actually going to need'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a separate accessory. This isn\'t the official one, this is just a workaround that they\'re using at the time from a separate brand\'s power bank. Their actual one will be an all-in-one device, so it\'ll just be one wire rather than multiple. But essentially, it magnetizes'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'onto your phone and then connects and powers the glasses so that your phone doesn\'t have to power them. This has the added benefit of keeping your phone from dying when you\'re using your screens. And plus, this entire thing is going to'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'magnetize onto the back of their case just like this third-party one did so that you can keep this entire kit all-in-one together. You\'re also getting virtual environments just like the Apple Vision Pro so you don\'t have to be in the real world. You can transform the'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'background of everything but the screens into a 360 view of a really cool new place like a movie theater, the desert at day or night, and more just like the Apple Vision Pro. Now, I actually asked about this specifically because I was'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really curious. Apple Vision Pro has 13 virtual environments now and I asked them, "How many do you guys actually have and are you planning on adding?" And they said, "Right now they have three to four virtual environments but they\'re planning to add as many as'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'possible." In addition, they\'re also planning to open this up with some sort of conversion kit or developer kit so that you or whoever wants to can add whatever virtual environments imaginable. Now, I\'m going to stop'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'myself right here for a second. I know right now a lot of you are asking about the prescriptions. So, for you guys, can I even use these if I wear glasses? Do they support them? Do they have built-in adjustments like some AR glasses? Yeah, they do to both of those. They have'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'individual adjustment knobs for diopters for each eye built into the glasses for minor prescriptions and they support bigger prescriptions as well which you\'ll be able to buy third-party. Now, capability-wise, you can do seven mixed'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'screens on your computer, three screens on your phone, and you can charge your phone while using them with the separate device that I mentioned earlier that they\'re working on. And now you know you\'re getting virtual environments too'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and potentially an endless amount of them. Your screen can be placed anywhere, 360° all the way around you, and resized too just like the Apple Vision Pro. But, what else are you getting? Well, because these have 6° of freedom, 6 DoF, and SLAM, you can also'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'watch 360 videos, 180° videos, and yeah, I know a lot of you guys are wondering about Steam VR support from this 93 g pair of glasses. And I asked about this directly again because I wanted more'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'answers. And here\'s what the CEO said. Basically, it is a primary goal of theirs and software doesn\'t actually seem to be a problem at all. Their big thing right now that they need to figure'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'out is how to find a provider, a third-party controller that\'s going to work with their system. Because as you know, probably with Steam VR games, you need those controllers to actually control yourself virtual environment that you can walk around. So that\'s sort'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the main thing they need to figure out. Again, they don\'t think the software is going to be an issue at all because it already seems highly possible with the actual hardware that they already have. Additionally, Steam VR compatibility probably won\'t be'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'available on launch, but since everyone is asking about it and they\'ve now made it a major goal of theirs to pursue this, possible that it could become up and running after launch at some point. Which wow, that\'s really exciting. It took years for Apple Vision Pro to get'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any controller support or any sort of gaming support whatsoever. But since Unseen Reality is actually making this priority, it\'s probably going to come a lot a lot faster. Especially with how much attention these are getting and how'

[06:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'popular Steam VR as a possibility already is. Okay, so let\'s slow down. I got to also test these for hours. What did I think of the actual hands-on testing? Well, first let\'s talk about the controls. There\'s a couple different'

[07:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'buttons on them and essentially the functionality of this is it allows you to re-center your screen in front of you, switch between four different screen sizes just like sort of default so you don\'t have to do that hand'

[07:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tracking or positioning manually. You can just press the button and basically the four default settings it has for screen size right now, if you want to do that quick access, is close and small, far and small, far and large, and then also close and large, which 90° field of'

[07:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'view, oh my gosh, you have to crank your entire head to actually see the full screen if if watching a movie or something. So most of the time I have it on the far and large setting. You really'

[07:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t get how big 90° field of view is until you actually have it with like a movie screen and you literally can\'t see the entire thing without turning your whole head. It\'s honestly incredible and it\'s extremely close to what VR headsets'

[07:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are working with. You can also black out the background if you want to do that and because it\'s virtual see-through, it\'s like literally fully pitch black, which is really nice. You can switch between that and clear. Hand tracking, how does that actually work? Well, it'

[08:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'basically works how you would expect it to. You can move screens around by grabbing that little bar underneath them just like with the Apple Vision Pro and then you can resize them as well, place them in whatever part of your workspace or play space that you want. So, I tried'

[08:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the hand tracking with their little Windows laptop they had here set up for the demos along with all the other YouTubers there. I also tried watching shows and movies and YouTube videos cast directly from my'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'phone onto the glasses and I even played some video games with a gaming handheld. And from those three sort of different demo experiences, what did I notice and how was my experience? The pros and the'

[08:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cons. The first pro is the clear bright world background and completely opaque digital screen, something that traditional AR glasses just aren\'t able to accomplish. They dim the background in the best case scenarios and when you'

[09:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have a screen when it\'s really bright outside, it isn\'t fully opaque. It\'s transparent. You can see through it the brighter it is actually outside. So, if they don\'t support an enormous amount of nits, there\'s going to be conditions where you\'re not going to fully be able'

[09:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to see the details of the screen and it\'s going to be drowned out by the background. The URXR ones, however, don\'t have that problem at all because it\'s completely virtual. Again, the screens are crystal clear and completely opaque, meaning you can\'t see through'

[09:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them whatsoever no matter the lighting conditions. Whether it\'s inside in the dark or outside in the glaring sun with white concrete all around you, it doesn\'t matter. It\'s always going to be crystal clear. Second, this is a con,'

[09:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these don\'t actually have speakers. So, you have to either wear earbuds or over-ear headphones, which is my preference, or you can use the audio from your connected device, like directly from your phone or directly from your computer playing out of the'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'speakers if you want. Now, this is one of my complaints personally, but honestly, every time I use AR glasses for any longish period of time, I always use my over-ear headphones anyways. The sound quality\'s way better, the immersion\'s way better, and it\'s just'

[10:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really unmatched as far as the experience that you\'re getting. But, this is a notable con if no speakers is a deal breaker for you if you don\'t want to carry around headphones. Third, and this is a pro, is screen size. Holy heck on Earth, the screen with 90° field of'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'view is massive. When it\'s at max, like I said, I literally can\'t see the entire thing without turning my entire head. So, it\'s perfect for multiple screens because of that. Like, when you\'re watching a movie, you\'re not going to have it on max size. You\'re going to'

[10:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'push it back a little bit, keep it a little bit smaller. But, when you have multiple screens, it allows you to have all those and still be able to see all them. And you can just turn your eyes to look at the different ones rather than having to turn your whole head to get that field of view in like you would'

[10:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with other AR glasses. The next pro is going to be screen quality. So, this is 2.5K per eye instead of 1080 or 1200p like all the other AR glasses max out at. That\'s the resolution per eye. As for the screen output, the demo version'

[10:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that we used outputted at 1080, so it\'d be a direct comparison against other AR glasses. While the launch version is going to output at 1440p, and that\'s huge. It really makes a big difference. That\'s a big quality jump. Now, I'

[11:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mentioned the one con being the no speakers, but there actually is another pretty big con right now. And that is the hand tracking. It just isn\'t as good as it should be. The nice thing about this is when we got to this event, they'

[11:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'literally told all of us YouTubers, "Please tell us everything that is like wrong with this. Like, your worst complaints, give us all of them so that we can fix it." That\'s the main thing they were looking for so that they they spend a lot of time improving those'

[11:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things that are wrong with them, which honestly I love. And all of us had the complaint of like the hand tracking not being as good as we wanted it to be. If they listen to us, it\'s going to be a lot better when launch actually comes out. Now lastly, as a con, is the'

[11:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'appearance. These are pretty futuristic looking, more so than some smaller AR glasses. So that could be a pretty big con for you if you haven\'t bought AR glasses at all because you want them to look like normal glasses. These just'

[11:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aren\'t going to be for you. They look futuristic like of other AR glasses. So if you\'re looking for the best style, these probably won\'t push you over the hump if you\'ve never bought AR glasses before. But if you\'re used to the look of AR glasses, this isn\'t super outside of the realm of that. And it\'s basically'

[12:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like Apple Vision Pro at a fraction of the cost. So is that worth it to you to get all the functionality? Well, that\'s up to you. These are $799 right now if you didn\'t happen to get the pre-order discount for $699. The retail launch price will be starting at $899. But if'

[12:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you want to check them out, you can. I\'m really excited for the next time I get to try them out. Overall, based on this whole experience, the CEO, the team, they\'re all really nice. They seem to be actively seeking out as much criticism as possible, which is a great sign. Not'

[12:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a lot of companies do that. And their product can already do so much more than the AR glasses competition. It\'s kind of shocking. What do you guys think? Is this a product you\'re willing to check out? Will you buy it or will you pass? And why? Let all of us know all of your'

